<TopicPage slug="subjects/gender-studies/foundational/sex-gender-and-sexuality-the-vocabulary">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is the first lesson of Gender Studies, and it needs no prior lessons — only words
    you already use and a willingness to have them examined. The field begins by taking
    apart vocabulary everyone uses daily and assumes everyone means the same thing by, so
    this page is where every later conversation in the course starts.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>define sex, gender and sexuality as the field uses the terms, and say why the distinctions are drawn;</li>
    <li>separate gender identity, gender expression and gender roles as independent axes rather than one variable;</li>
    <li>state what intersex variations are and why they complicate "biology is exactly binary";</li>
    <li>explain social constructionism — including the three qualifications that keep it honest — against essentialism;</li>
    <li>show the vocabulary doing analytical work, using the gender pay gap as a worked example.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    One habit throughout: when a claim about gender is made, ask <em>which</em> concept it
    is about — bodies, roles or desire. Most public arguments about gender are two people
    using one word for three different things.
  </p>

  <h2>The tick-box problem</h2>
  <p>
    Consider a form you have almost certainly filled in. It asks for your name, birthdate
    and "gender" — but it wants a tick-box with two options, so it is really asking for
    your sex as recorded at birth, while using the word "gender" for the asking. In
    ordinary English the three terms blur: people say "gender" when they mean chromosomes,
    "orientation" when they mean identity. Nothing goes wrong on the form. Things go wrong
    the moment we try to reason — because with blurred concepts, no precise question can
    even be asked. Which differences between people track bodies? Which track societies?
    Which track desire? You cannot begin to answer until the concepts are separated — in
    the same way physics could not begin until it separated weight from mass.
  </p>
  <p>
    This page builds the working vocabulary in three passes — the sex/gender distinction,
    sexuality and identity terms, and the field's central theoretical debate, social
    construction versus essentialism — then shows the vocabulary doing real analytical
    work. The widget below is the map of the whole lesson.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the foundational distinctions" widget="equationStep" config={vocabCfg} />

  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> steps 1–3 are three separate axes, not one variable
    in three outfits; step 4 is the field's core theoretical claim; and step 5 is the
    point of the exercise — the distinctions are not etiquette, they are instruments. Keep
    asking, for every claim you meet: which step is this sentence actually about?
  </p>

  <h2>Distinguishing sex and gender</h2>
  <p>
    In the field's working vocabulary, <strong>sex</strong> refers to the biological
    attributes: chromosomes, gonads, hormone profiles and anatomy. Anatomists separate
    <em>primary</em> sex characteristics (the reproductive organs themselves) from
    <em>secondary</em> ones (breasts, facial hair, voice pitch — features shaped by
    hormones but not directly reproductive). The textbook binary — typically
    <em>female</em>: two X chromosomes, ovaries, higher oestrogen; <em>male</em>: an X and
    a Y, testes, higher testosterone — describes the majority well. But the word
    "majority" is doing work in that sentence, and the field insists on noticing it:
    <strong>intersex</strong> variations exist, in which chromosomes, hormones or anatomy
    do not fit the standard binary pattern. How often depends entirely on definition —
    strict estimates (for instance, chromosomal variations requiring clinical attention)
    run well under one birth in a thousand, while broader counts that include milder
    hormonal variations run between one and two in a hundred, comparable to being a
    natural redhead. The analytic point is not the exact figure; it is that biological sex
    is a distribution with tails, not a clean switch.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "sex is always visibly either male or female — you
  can always tell." It seems self-evident from everyday life, where almost everyone you
  meet sorts cleanly. But chromosomes and hormones are invisible without testing, many
  intersex variations are discovered by accident (or never), and secondary
  characteristics overlap heavily across the binary. The correct picture: the
  <em>usual</em> pattern is bimodal and strongly predictive, but the assignment itself —
  "sex assigned at birth", a phrase used clinically long before it was politically
  contested — is an interpretation made from external signs, and the tails are real.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>Gender</strong> is everything social built on that biological ground: the
    roles, expectations, norms, symbols and identities a society attaches to being seen as
    a woman, a man or something else. What counts as masculine or feminine is strikingly
    unstable. Pink was marketed for boys and blue for girls in early twentieth-century
    America — the switch came mid-century. Trousers were scandalous on women a century ago
    and are unremarkable now — while garments close to skirts or robes count as masculine
    dress across much of the Middle East, Asia and Africa, and nobody calls a kilt-wearing
    Scotsman feminine. Which jobs are "men's work" flips across cultures and decades:
    teaching and clerical work were male-dominated professions when they required literacy
    and authority, then became female-typed as wages and status fell. If the mapping from
    bodies to roles were natural law, it would not need this much maintenance — schools,
    advertising, law and family life all work continuously to produce and enforce it,
    which is itself evidence of how much is social.
  </p>
  <p>
    The distinction then splits into finer instruments. <strong>Gender identity</strong>
    is a person's internal sense of their own gender — for most people aligned with the
    sex assigned at birth (<strong>cisgender</strong>), for <strong>transgender</strong>
    people not; the umbrella also gathers agender, genderfluid and genderqueer
    identities, among others. <strong>Gender expression</strong> is how one presents:
    dress, movement, voice — which may or may not match expectations for one's identity,
    and ranges from conventional to <strong>androgynous</strong>, blending what a culture
    codes masculine and feminine. <strong>Gender roles</strong> are society's scripts: who
    is expected to provide care, to initiate, to lead, to defer. A person can be a
    cisgender woman with a masculine expression, or a transgender man with a conventional
    one; the concepts are separate axes, not one variable. The vocabulary entered research
    in the 1950s–60s (John Money's "gender role", Robert Stoller's <em>Sex and
    Gender</em>), initially in clinical contexts, and it has done its work ever since by
    letting researchers ask, for any observed difference, <em>which</em> variable explains
    it.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> that "gender is a social construct" means bodies and
  biology are imaginary, or that the field denies physiological difference. The claim is
  narrower and harder to dismiss: bodies are real, but their <em>meanings</em> are made.
  No society disputes that pregnancy happens to female bodies; societies differ enormously
  on what a pregnant person may therefore be paid, whether she may study, drive, appear
  unveiled, or inherit — and those differences are the constructed part, and the part
  where inequality lives.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> three words pulled apart — sex as biology (with real tails),
    gender as the social architecture built on it (with wobbly, maintained walls), and the
    finer axes of identity, expression and roles. The next section adds the third axis:
    desire.
  </p>

  <h2>Sexuality and identity: a separate axis</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Sexuality</strong> — desire, attraction and orientation — is a distinct
    dimension from gender identity, and the field's broadest definition takes in the whole
    range of its expression: thoughts, fantasies, desires, behaviours, roles and
    relationships, with biological, emotional, social and spiritual aspects. One of the
    field's earliest findings is that this axis is statistically independent of gender
    identity. Knowing that someone is a man says little about whether he is attracted to
    men, women or both; knowing someone is transgender says nothing necessary about their
    orientation; a trans woman attracted to women, a cisgender man attracted to men and a
    lesbian all share a same-sex orientation while differing completely in gender identity.
    The vocabulary keeps the categories from bleeding into each other.
  </p>
  <p>
    Orientation terms are descriptive labels for patterns of attraction:
    <strong>heterosexual</strong> (a different gender), <strong>homosexual</strong> or
    gay/lesbian (the same gender), <strong>bisexual</strong> (more than one),
    <strong>pansexual</strong> (attraction regardless of gender), <strong>asexual</strong>
    (little or no sexual attraction — a distinct orientation, not a dysfunction). Two
    refinements matter analytically. First, researchers distinguish <em>attraction</em>,
    <em>behaviour</em> and <em>identity</em>: population surveys repeatedly find that the
    share of people reporting any same-sex attraction or behaviour exceeds the share who
    adopt an LGB identity, because identity is also a social and cultural act — it
    requires a vocabulary, a community and usually some safety. Second, the modern
    umbrella <strong>LGBTQ+</strong> (lesbian, gay, bisexual, transgender, queer, plus
    others) deliberately gathers people whose common ground is shared stigma rather than
    shared identity — which is precisely why the analytic distinctions stay necessary
    inside the coalition.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "gender identity and sexuality go together — feminine
  men must be gay, and trans people are just 'really' straight or gay in disguise." It
  seems reasonable because stereotypes bundle the two axes so tightly in popular imagery.
  But the independence is empirical: every combination of gender identity and orientation
  exists, and conflating them erases most of the people the vocabulary was built to
  describe — the trans woman attracted to women, the straight trans man, the asexual
  person of any gender. Three axes, three separate questions.</p>
  <p>
    The cross-cultural record is the strongest evidence that both gender and sexuality are
    socially organised. South Asian <em>hijra</em> communities are legally recognised as a
    third gender in India, Nepal and Bangladesh; Samoan <em>fa'afafine</em> are accepted as
    an integrated gender category; many Indigenous North American nations recognised (and
    the term <em>Two-Spirit</em>, adopted in 1990, now gathers) people who filled neither
    standard role. The existence of institutionalised non-binary genders is not a modern
    invention — it is a standing demonstration that "how many genders are there?" is a
    question societies answer differently, and that the binary answer is one cultural
    convention among several, not a census of nature.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> three independent axes — sex (biology with tails), gender
    identity and expression (socially organised, culturally variable), sexuality (its own
    axis entirely). Now the field's central theoretical question: what kind of thing is
    gender, given all this variation?
  </p>

  <h2>Social construction versus essentialism: the field's core debate</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Essentialism</strong> is the position that categories like "woman" and "man"
    have a fixed, natural essence that determines their members' traits and destinies —
    "men are naturally competitive, women naturally nurturing" is the paradigm sentence.
    The field's dominant counter-position, <strong>social constructionism</strong>, holds
    that the meanings of gender categories are produced by social processes and therefore
    vary across cultures and change over time. Three qualifications keep the debate
    honest. Constructionism does not claim biology is irrelevant — hormones and
    physiology influence tendencies, and the field has accepted this increasingly since
    biologists like Anne Fausto-Sterling insisted that the real biology is itself more
    variable and interactive than the cartoon. It does not claim everything is chosen or
    infinitely malleable — individuals experience gender as deeply felt and often
    immovable. And it does not claim constructs are unreal: money is a social construct
    and remains entirely real in its effects. A construct, in the social-scientific sense,
    is something that exists <em>because</em> societies maintain it — which is exactly
    what makes it analysable, criticisable and changeable in a way that natural law is
    not.
  </p>
  <p>
    Judith Butler's <em>Gender Trouble</em> (1990) radicalised the position with the
    claim that gender is <em>performative</em>: not a fixed identity that expression
    merely reveals, but an identity continuously produced by the very repeated acts —
    dress, gesture, speech — that seem to express it, the way "standing" is produced by
    continuously standing rather than revealed by it. Judith Lorber's <em>Paradoxes of
    Gender</em> (1994) gives the sociological version: a society constantly "does gender"
    — in bathrooms, sports categories, names, dress — and then treats the resulting
    pattern as evidence of nature. The empirical work since has consistently favoured the
    constructionist account wherever the two make different predictions: roles vary across
    cultures (essentialism predicts constancy), gender-typing shifts within generations
    (essentialism predicts stability), and intersex and transgender experience shows
    biology itself refusing the tidy categories the essentialist argument requires.
  </p>
  <p>
    What the distinction buys is visible in one worked example: the gender pay gap. Take
    the widely cited US figure that full-time working women earn roughly 80–85 cents per
    dollar paid to men. Essentialism reads it as one fact about natural difference.
    Decomposition analysis — which is only possible once sex, gender roles and choice-sets
    are kept conceptually distinct — un-bundles it: part is hours and interruptions
    (gendered: caregiving still falls disproportionately on women); part is occupational
    segregation (which jobs are coded "women's" and paid less — a social pattern that
    flips when wages and status change); part is within-job pay differences (partly
    discrimination, partly negotiation structures); part is explained by measured factors.
    Each component has a different remedy — parental leave policy, pay-transparency law,
    promotion audit — and a politics that treats the gap as one undifferentiated fact of
    nature has, in effect, chosen to remedy none of them.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "the sex/gender distinction is academic
  hair-splitting — everyone knows what the words mean, and the vocabulary changes
  nothing." It seems reasonable because the distinctions are invisible when they are
  working. But the pay-gap example shows the opposite: without separating bodies from
  roles from desire, the gap is one unanalysable blob, and every remedy is a guess.
  With the distinctions, it is four addressable problems. The vocabulary is not
  pedantry; it is the difference between a question that can be answered and one that
  cannot.</p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Define sex, gender and sexuality in one sentence each, in the field's usage.</li>
    <li>Gender identity, gender expression and gender roles are three different axes. Describe one person (real or hypothetical) for whom the three point in different directions.</li>
    <li>Why does the intersex case matter to the argument, even at low frequencies?</li>
    <li>What do the hijra, fa'afafine and Two-Spirit categories demonstrate about the question "how many genders are there?"</li>
    <li>State one essentialist prediction and one constructionist prediction about gender roles across cultures — and say which the evidence favours.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Sex: the biological attributes — chromosomes, hormones,
    anatomy, primary and secondary characteristics. Gender: the social roles, norms,
    symbols and identities a society attaches to being seen as a woman, a man or another
    category. Sexuality: the axis of desire, attraction and orientation, independent of
    the other two. (2) For example: a cisgender man (identity aligned with assignment)
    whose expression is androgynous and who does most of the caregiving in his family (a
    role reversal) — or a trans woman with a conventionally feminine expression. Any
    example where the three axes separate works; the point is that none is deducible from
    the others. (3) Because it shows biological sex itself is a distribution with tails
    rather than a clean switch, any claim built on "biology is exactly binary" has to
    survive the tails — and the broader prevalence estimates (between one and two in a
    hundred) are not rare at all. (4) That the number of genders is something societies
    decide and institutionalise differently; the binary is one convention among several,
    not a census of nature. (5) Essentialism predicts constancy — the same roles wherever
    humans are found; constructionism predicts variation across cultures and shifts over
    time. The record — pink/blue reversals, trousers, men's work flipping, third genders —
    favours the constructionist account, while its three qualifications (biology matters,
    experience is deeply felt, constructs are real) keep it honest.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Sex (biology), gender (social meanings) and sexuality (desire) are three analytically separate axes — the field's foundational move.</li>
    <li>Biological sex is a distribution with tails: intersex variations exist, from well under one in a thousand (strict definitions) to between one and two in a hundred (broad ones).</li>
    <li>Gender varies enormously across cultures and eras — pink/blue, trousers, "men's work", third genders — which is evidence of construction, not natural law.</li>
    <li>Identity, expression and roles are separate axes; cisgender and transgender describe identity, not expression or orientation.</li>
    <li>Orientation is independent of gender identity; attraction, behaviour and identity are themselves distinct, which is why survey numbers differ.</li>
    <li>Social constructionism does not deny bodies: it claims their meanings are made — and a construct can be entirely real, analysable and changeable.</li>
    <li>Butler's performativity and Lorber's "doing gender" explain how the construction is continuously maintained — and then mistaken for nature.</li>
    <li>The vocabulary pays for itself: decomposing the pay gap into distinct causes is only possible once the concepts are distinct — and each cause has a different remedy.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The next lesson, <em>Feminist Movements in History</em>, turns from concepts to the
    people who organised around them — suffrage, liberation and the waves in between.
    <em>Classical Feminist Theory</em> adds Beauvoir and the classics; <em>Gender and the
    Economy</em> takes the pay gap you just saw decomposed and gives it a full lesson;
    and <em>Intersectional and Queer Theory</em> develops the two ideas left at the edge
    of this page — Crenshaw's intersectionality and Butler's performativity — in the depth
    they deserve. The vocabulary you built here is the entry fee to all of them.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Three refinements carry the vocabulary toward intermediate analysis.
    <strong>Intersectionality</strong> (Kimberlé Crenshaw, 1989) observes that gender
    never operates alone: a Black woman, a working-class woman and a disabled woman
    experience "womanhood" through different interlocking structures, and single-axis
    analysis systematically misses those who sit at the crossings — Crenshaw's original
    cases were women of colour who fell between the protections of race discrimination law
    and sex discrimination law. <strong>Doing gender</strong> (West and Zimmerman, 1987)
    reframes gender as a routine social accomplishment — something people continuously
    <em>do</em> and are held accountable for, in every interaction, which explains the
    social cost of ambiguity and the policing of "gender nonconformity". And the
    biological nuance keeps deepening: endocrine and neuroscientific work increasingly
    studies variation within sexes rather than mean differences between them, while the
    concept of "sex assigned at birth" — used clinically long before it became politically
    contested — reminds us that even the assignment act is an interpretation made in a
    social world. The field's through-line since its founding is constant: gender is a
    lens on power — who gets to be what, who is entitled to what, and who pays for the
    maintenance of the categories — and its analysis is empirical as well as political.
  </p>

